            <mri:abstract>
                <gco:CharacterString>Aerosol concentrations in the Southern Hemisphere are largely undersampled. This study presents a chemical and physical description of dust particles collected on board research vessels in the southeast Pacific (SEPS) and the Southern Ocean (SOKS). Concentrations of dust were 6.1 &#177; 2.4 ng m&#8315;&#179; for SEPS and 13.0 &#177; 6.3 ng m&#8315;&#179; for SOKS. Dust fluxes, derived from those concentrations, were 9.9 &#177; 3.7 &#956;g m&#8315;&#179; d&#8315;&#179; for SEPS and 38 &#177; 14 &#956;g m&#8315;&#179; d&#8315;&#179; for SOKS and are shown to be representative of actual fluxes in those areas. Dust and iron deposition are up to 2 orders of magnitude lower than former predictions. A map of dust deposition on the Southern Hemisphere is proposed by incorporating those in situ measurements into a dust model. This study confirms that dust deposition is not the dominant source of iron to the large high-nutrient low-chlorophyll Southern Ocean.</gco:CharacterString>
            </mri:abstract>
